{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And there was evening and there was morning, one day.</b> Why was it not said 'first day'? Because our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 11a): one mentions the attribute of day at night and the attribute of night at day, to remove from the heart of the heretics who say that from one beginning two opposites could not have emerged, and therefore they decreed to say: He who created light did not create darkness. Therefore it says 'and there was evening and there was morning' — that is, night and day; and even though they appear as opposites, nevertheless the two of them are 'one day,' the day of the One, for one El created them both. And this explanation is more lucid than the explanation of Rashi, who explained that it comes to indicate that the Holy One, blessed be He, was one — that the angels were not created until the second day — for angels are not mentioned here at all, and what does this matter have to do with the statement 'and there was evening and there was morning'?"], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:32:26.814788335Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}